Model routing for new swarm workers:

Roles and cost
- GPT 6.1 Sol is the default main chat and coordinator, with chat effort high. The saved worker default is openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol with effort low.
- X5 code-large is the preferred free workhorse for well-specified implementation, tests, refactors, bounded discovery and routine execution. Spawn a fresh worker with model "x5-copilot:x5-airun-code-large" and effort "max". The service identity is configured locally. Public backend equivalence is unverified.
- For paid swarm workers always explicitly use effort "low". Do not use none, medium, high, xhigh or max for paid workers. The main chat's high setting is separate and does not authorize high-effort workers.
- Use a fresh openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol worker with effort "low" for independent substantive review and paid tasks when X5 is unavailable or inappropriate. Use openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra with effort "low" only when it offers a task-specific advantage. The user's observed Terra high/xhigh cost on simple tasks can exceed Sol at lower effort; do not generalize that observation into a universal price comparison.

Choose by uncertainty and risk
- X5 handles bounded features, reproduced bug fixes, test implementation, fixed-interface refactors, adapters and other changes with clear acceptance criteria. Split larger work into testable slices.
- Sol coordinates ambiguous requirements, design, diagnosis and integration. Give independent reviewers enough context and use explicit positive and negative acceptance checks, especially for authorization, sensitive data, concurrency, durable serialization and transactions. A green unit test alone is not production acceptance.
- Terra low is an alternative for work where it has a concrete advantage. Do not automatically escalate effort to medium/high for complex work. Split the task, validate separately, or ask the user before changing the policy.
- For salary semantics, tenant/authentication/replay guards and durable legacy serialization, use independently authored positive and negative acceptance tests plus independent Sol review. Do not let an X5 worker define its own safety contract and be its only verifier.

Delegate with a contract
Provide the goal, expected behavior, owned paths and interfaces, invariants, forbidden side effects, a reproduction or reference, acceptance commands and edge cases. Distinguish new-object behavior from legacy roundtrip compatibility. One writer per worktree, isolate parallel writers.

Verify and escalate
Read each diff and independently check acceptance evidence before integration. Do not weaken supplied tests or treat a worker report as verification. Preserve negative controls. Allow one focused repair when failure has a clear local diagnosis. Escalate missing design or repeated failures to the coordinator, and use independent Sol low review where risk warrants it. Report externally blocked checks.

Routing mechanics
Pass both explicit model and effort on each fresh spawn or spawn-capable assignment. The local pre-tool gate rejects missing model or effort when active, but Jcode hooks fail open on errors and can be disabled. A reused worker keeps its model and effort regardless of assignment parameters. Use a fresh worker when the model or effort matters, without disrupting existing work. An explicit model wins over defaults; model "inherit" selects the coordinator and is not appropriate for normal routing. If X5 is unavailable, use Sol low and report the fallback. Cost claims should use observed route usage, retries and completed acceptance, not token counts treated as a public API invoice.

Privacy and structure
Keep endpoints, keys, private routing hosts, benchmark copies and results outside tracked repositories and public logs. Follow project disclosure rules. Always pass a label when spawning. In normal/light mode only the root spawns workers. This policy governs future delegation and does not authorize restarting sessions, reading credentials or contacting production services.
